{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#2460","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#2460","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#2460\",\"parent_revision\":\"a639abe389e9049d00b1df93e5e0968bfcf8492b19b75bad0045e40323d2a7ce\",\"root_revision\":\"a639abe389e9049d00b1df93e5e0968bfcf8492b19b75bad0045e40323d2a7ce\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: \\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: closed\\nadded: \\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nThe Final Handoff Output step runs `backlog_list(title=\\\"{slug}\\\")` where `{slug}` is derived from the completed feature's task file path. After `backlog_resolve` closed the current item, this search returned `count: 0`. The procedure then routes to `NothingQueued`, which opens the interactive browser showing all open P0/P1 items.\\n\\nDuring the preceding implementation run, two concerns were logged to issue #2437 via `backlog_groom`: a gap in `feature-researcher.md`'s SOP and a naming inconsistency in the Phase 2 STATUS block. These concerns are candidates for follow-on work but were not surfaced by the final handoff because they don't share the completed item's slug in their titles.\\n\\nThe final handoff's slug search is designed to find items that are direct successors of the completed work (same feature, same slug prefix). It has no mechanism to surface items that originated from concerns logged during the run.\\n\\nObserved: `complete-implementation` Final Handoff Output step, session 2026-05-25, issue #2437.\\n\\n## RT-ICA\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nRT-ICA Final: feat: complete-implementation final handoff slug search doesn't surface concern-derived follow-on work items\\nDate: 2026-05-25\\nGoal: Augment the Final Handoff Output step to read the resolved issue's Concerns section and surface non-empty entries to the developer alongside the existing slug-based search.\\nConditions:\\n1. Location of Final Handoff Output step | Snapshot: DERIVABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: fact-checker \u2014 final-handoff.md line 13 (backlog_list slug search), lines 10-23 (no Concerns read present)\\n2. Format of Concerns section entries | Snapshot: DERIVABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: fact-checker \u2014 concerns-processing.md line 22 (backlog_groom section=\\\"Concerns\\\")\\n3. Resolved issue number available at final handoff | Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: confirmed \u2014 issue number in scope throughout complete-implementation run\\n4. backlog_view supports section-filtered reads | Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: fact-checker \u2014 backlog_core/server.py function signature\\n5. Current slug search behavior when count=0 (NothingQueued route) | Snapshot: DERIVABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: impact-analyst \u2014 final-handoff.md lines 10-23 describe complete step flow\\n6. Primary change target: final-handoff.md (not SKILL.md inline) | Snapshot: (new) \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: impact-analyst \u2014 SKILL.md:718 is a reference pointer; final-handoff.md is the executable instruction\\n7. Documentation requiring update: backlog-item-lifecycle.md P6_FINAL section | Snapshot: (new) \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: impact-analyst \u2014 CONTENT_UPDATE risk MEDIUM\\nChanges from snapshot:\\n- Conditions 1, 2, 5: DERIVABLE \u2192 AVAILABLE (resolved by fact-checker + impact-analyst)\\n- Conditions 6, 7: (new) AVAILABLE (discovered by impact-analyst)\\nAVAILABLE count: 7\\nDERIVABLE count: 0\\nMISSING count: 0\\nDecision: APPROVED\\n</div>\\n\\n## Impact Radius\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n### Code \u2014 Producers (write the changed interface)\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/complete-implementation/references/final-handoff.md` \u2014 Owns the authoritative Mermaid flowchart for Final Handoff Output. The procedure will be augmented: insert a `backlog_view(selector='#{issue}', section='Concerns')` node before the existing `backlog_list(title='{slug}')` call; if Concerns section has non-empty entries, surface them to the developer; then continue to the existing slug-based routing. This is the only file with executable instruction content that changes. | Risk: HIGH | Why: Primary AI instruction artifact being modified. Flowchart is treated as an executable process map \u2014 ambiguity or ordering errors produce incorrect agent behavior. One additional MCP call (backlog_view) and one new conditional branch added.\\n\\n### Code \u2014 Consumers (read the changed interface)\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/complete-implementation/SKILL.md` \u2014 Delegates to `final-handoff.md` at line 718 via `Execute the full procedure defined in [./references/final-handoff.md]`. The delegation is a reference pointer, not inline logic; the augmented procedure is automatically consumed. Frontmatter description does not specifically enumerate final handoff steps. | Risk: LOW | Why: No inline duplication of final-handoff logic in SKILL.md \u2014 the reference transparently inherits the updated procedure.\\n\\n### Code \u2014 Other References\\nNone identified.\\n\\n### Tests\\nNone identified. The complete-implementation skill is an AI-executable workflow document (Mermaid flowchart), not a Python callable with unit tests. No test file covers the Final Handoff Output routing logic. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: Behavioral correctness of the augmented procedure can only be verified by running the skill end-to-end; no automated coverage exists for the concerns-surfacing branch.\\n\\n### Documentation\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/backlog-item-lifecycle.md` \u2014 The P6_FINAL Mermaid node (line 596) describes the final handoff as only \\\"Final commit and push / Final Handoff Output: 'Clear context and run: /dh:work-backlog-item <next-item>'\\\". The node contract table (line 635) shows P6_FINAL with empty inputs and \\\"terminal\\\" edge. After this change, both become stale: P6_FINAL has a new inputs field (resolved issue's Concerns section) and is no longer a simple terminal \u2014 it reads `backlog_view` first. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: Documentation used by future plan architects and agents to understand the complete-implementation pipeline; stale P6_FINAL contract understates the handoff behavior and would mislead agents reasoning about what the step does.\\n\\n### Configuration / CI\\nNone identified.\\n\\n### Agent Instructions\\nNone identified. No agent instruction file (`plugins/development-harness/agents/*.md`) describes what happens at the Final Handoff Output step. The step is fully contained within the skill's reference files.\\n\\n### Systems Inventory\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/complete-implementation/references/final-handoff.md` | Role: producer | Connection: Contains the only authoritative procedure for Final Handoff Output \u2014 the Mermaid flowchart that agents execute | Action: CODE_CHANGE | Risk: HIGH | pattern: 'NothingQueued'\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/complete-implementation/SKILL.md` | Role: consumer | Connection: Line 718 delegates to final-handoff.md via reference pointer \u2014 the augmented procedure is transparently inherited | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: LOW\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/backlog-item-lifecycle.md` | Role: documentation | Connection: P6_FINAL Mermaid node (line 596) and node contract table (line 635) describe the Final Handoff step and will be stale after augmentation | Action: CONTENT_UPDATE | Risk: MEDIUM | pattern: 'P6_FINAL'\\n\\n### Risk Summary\\n- Overall system risk: LOW\\n- Highest-risk systems:\\n  - `plugins/development-harness/skills/complete-implementation/references/final-handoff.md` \u2014 sole executable procedure for Final Handoff; any ambiguity in the new Mermaid node produces incorrect routing\\n  - `plugins/development-harness/docs/backlog-item-lifecycle.md` \u2014 P6_FINAL node and contract become stale documentation if not updated alongside the reference file\\n- Main risk themes:\\n  - behavioral (new conditional branch in the Mermaid flowchart must handle both empty and non-empty Concerns)\\n  - documentation (backlog-item-lifecycle.md will understate Final Handoff behavior)\\n- Scope expansion:\\n  - None. All affected systems were within the original item description. The change is precisely scoped to `final-handoff.md` (primary) and one documentation file.\\n\\n### Ecosystem Completeness Checklist\\n- [x] Every code producer updated or verified compatible\\n- [x] Every code consumer migrated or verified compatible\\n- [ ] Every affected test updated or new coverage added \u2014 no automated tests exist; manual/integration verification required\\n- [ ] Every stale document updated \u2014 `backlog-item-lifecycle.md` P6_FINAL node and contract require content update\\n- [x] Every affected config or CI path updated and validated\\n- [x] Every affected agent or skill instruction updated\\n- [x] Replacement or migration coverage gaps resolved \u2014 not a migration item\\n- [x] Hidden transitive dependencies checked \u2014 no additional consumers or producers found\\n</div>\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer using Claude Code skills**, I want to **complete-implementation final handoff slug search doesn't surface concern-derived follow-on work items** so that **the tooling becomes more capable and complete**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session observation 2026-05-25 \u2014 complete-implementation run for issue #2437\\n- **Priority**: Ideas\\n- **Added**: 2026-05-25\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n\\n<!-- artifact-gist:af51a3f2b0c758d9226861dc7f4f3214 -->\\n</div>\\n\\n## Groomed (2026-05-25)\\n\\n### Reproducibility\\n\\n1. Start a `complete-implementation` run on any backlog item that has a linked GitHub issue.\\n2. During the run, log at least one concern against the issue using `backlog_groom(selector=\\\"#{issue}\\\", section=\\\"Concerns\\\", content=\\\"Some concern text\\\")`.\\n3. Allow the run to reach the Final Handoff Output step, where the item is marked resolved via `backlog_resolve`.\\n4. Review the final handoff output presented to the developer.\\n5. Observe: slug-based follow-on search results appear (via `backlog_list(title=\\\"{slug}\\\")`).\\n6. Observe: the Concerns entries logged in step 2 are absent from the output \u2014 they are never read or surfaced.\\n\\n### Priority\\n\\nIdeas/10 \u2014 This is a quality-of-life gap, not a workflow blocker. `complete-implementation` runs complete without error regardless of whether Concerns are present. The missing behavior affects only runs where Concerns were explicitly recorded during execution, which is not the common case today.\\n\\nPriority rises to P2 or higher when teams routinely use the Concerns section to track mid-run discoveries. At that adoption level, silently dropping concerns at handoff becomes a repeatable information loss event rather than an occasional miss.\\n\\n### Impact\\n\\n- **Affected**: Developers using `complete-implementation` who log concerns during a run and expect them to surface at the handoff boundary.\\n- **Scenario**: A developer or agent logs `\\\"Concern: edge case X may regress\\\"` during a run. At Final Handoff Output, that concern does not appear. The developer must separately recall to open the resolved issue and inspect its Concerns section \u2014 a step with no prompt, reminder, or link in the current workflow.\\n- **Silent data loss**: The Concerns section is written by `backlog_groom` but never read by the Final Handoff step. The gap exists at every run; it is only visible when someone checks the resolved issue manually after the fact.\\n- **No hard block**: The workflow completes without error; the loss is invisible to the developer at the moment it occurs.\\n\\n### Benefits\\n\\n- Concerns logged during a run reach the developer at the moment of handoff, when implementation context is freshest and follow-up action is most likely.\\n- No manual step is required to inspect the resolved issue's Concerns section after completion \u2014 the handoff output is self-contained.\\n- The complete picture of follow-on work (slug-matched items and mid-run concerns) is visible in one place at one time.\\n- Concern entries that would otherwise be silently dropped become actionable at the handoff boundary rather than being discovered later or not at all.\\n\\n### Expected Behavior\\n\\nAt the Final Handoff Output step, after the resolved issue is identified, the agent reads the Concerns section of that issue.\\n\\n- **When the Concerns section is non-empty**: the concern entries are displayed to the developer as a labeled group, visually or structurally distinct from slug-search results, so the developer can tell which items came from recorded concerns versus the slug search.\\n- **When the Concerns section is absent or empty**: the output is identical to current behavior \u2014 no new empty block or label appears.\\n\\nThe developer sees all mid-run concerns surfaced in one place at handoff, without needing to separately open the resolved issue to find them. The slug-based search behavior is unchanged.\\n\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n1. Given a resolved issue with a non-empty Concerns section, the Final Handoff Output includes those Concerns entries in its displayed output.\\n2. Given a resolved issue with no Concerns section or an empty Concerns section, the Final Handoff Output is unchanged from current behavior \u2014 no new empty block or label appears in the output.\\n3. In the Final Handoff Output, Concerns entries are labeled or structured distinctly from slug-search results \u2014 a reader can identify which items came from the Concerns section versus the slug search without inspecting the source issue.\\n4. The `final-handoff.md` Mermaid flowchart contains a node that reads the resolved issue's Concerns section, and that node is positioned before the existing slug-search node.\\n5. After the change, the `SKILL.md` reference pointer to `final-handoff.md` (line 718) remains accurate and consistent with the updated file content.\\n\\n### Resources\\n\\n| Type | Item |\\n|------|------|\\n| Prior work | `plugins/development-harness/skills/complete-implementation/references/final-handoff.md` \u2014 lines 10\u201323: current Final Handoff flowchart; line 13: `backlog_list(title=\\\"{slug}\\\")` as sole search call (VERIFIED) |\\n| Prior work | `plugins/development-harness/skills/complete-implementation/references/concerns-processing.md` \u2014 line 22: `backlog_groom(section=\\\"Concerns\\\", ...)` write path (VERIFIED) |\\n| Prior work | `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py` \u2014 `backlog_view` section filter capability (VERIFIED) |\\n| Skill | /dh:complete-implementation |\\n\\n### Files\\n\\n| File | Change type | Detail |\\n|------|-------------|--------|\\n| `plugins/development-harness/skills/complete-implementation/references/final-handoff.md` | PRIMARY | Mermaid flowchart (lines 10\u201323) gains a new node for reading the resolved issue's Concerns section, placed before the existing slug-search step. This is the sole AI-executable instruction for the Final Handoff Output step \u2014 risk HIGH. |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/skills/complete-implementation/SKILL.md` | VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Reference pointer at line 718 must remain consistent with the updated `final-handoff.md`. No substantive content change expected; verify only \u2014 risk LOW. |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/docs/backlog-item-lifecycle.md` | CONTENT_UPDATE | P6_FINAL node description and associated table row describe the current slug-only handoff behavior and become stale after the primary change. Update to reflect the Concerns-read step \u2014 risk MEDIUM. |\\n\\n### Dependencies\\n\\n- Depends on: None\\n- Blocks: None\\n\\n### Effort\\n\\nSmall \u2014 3 markdown/SKILL.md files, no code changes. The primary change is a single new node in a Mermaid flowchart plus the instruction prose that follows it in `final-handoff.md`. Scope is tightly bounded by the confirmed impact radius: one primary file edit, one verify-compatible check, one content update to lifecycle docs.\\n\\n### Concerns\\n\\n- [ ] Q-1: Cross-document `#{N}` source inconsistency \u2014 `final-handoff.md` attributes the resolved issue number to \\\"Apply status:verified Label Step 2\\\" but `backlog-item-lifecycle.md` Node Contracts row says \\\"(from P6_RESOLVE)\\\". An agent reading the lifecycle doc looks in the wrong step. (reported by qg-T0-mpr on T0)\\n- [ ] Q-2: No null guard for `#{N}` before the `ConcernsCheck` `backlog_view` call \u2014 `ConcernsError` branch handles API errors but not a missing/null selector. (reported by qg-T0-mpr on T0)\\n\\n### Design Intent Alignment\\n\\n**Alignment assessment**: ALIGNED\\n**Mission citations**: The `complete-implementation` skill is designed to be the terminal quality gate for a feature run \u2014 its purpose is to surface all quality signals before handing off to the developer. Reading the Concerns section at final handoff extends this responsibility to include concerns logged during the run, which is consistent with the skill's stated purpose of comprehensive completion verification.\\n**Rationale**: Adding a Concerns-section check to the Final Handoff Output step is a minimal extension of existing behavior (one additional MCP call) that closes a silent data loss path. It does not change the architectural model, the backlog tooling interface, or the agent dispatch mechanism. The confirmed scope (completed issue only, surface not auto-create) keeps the change bounded and developer-controlled.\\n\\n### Grooming Notes\\n\\nValidation gate notes (2026-05-25, NARROW scope groom):\\n1. Section name normalization: RT-ICA (stored as rt_ica), Fact-Check (stored as fact_check), and Impact Radius subsections written as top-level sections are all present in YAML but do not appear in sections_index due to the same server normalization issue documented in #2459. Content is fully present in local YAML and synced to GitHub.\\n2. Issue Classification and Design Intent Alignment were written directly by the orchestrator (NARROW scope does not spawn classifier or alignment-analyst). Type: missing-guardrail, Alignment: ALIGNED.\\n\\n### Issue Classification\\n\\n**Type**: missing-guardrail\\n**Rationale**: The system (final handoff) allowed a bad outcome (concern-derived follow-on work silently dropped) that an existing gate (Final Handoff Output step) should have prevented. The gate runs after every implementation but does not check the Concerns section, which is the mechanism for logging quality issues during a run.\\n**Analysis Method**: No root-cause analysis needed for missing-guardrail (the absence of a check is the root cause).\\n**Scenario Target**: complete-implementation run that logs concerns via backlog_groom \u2192 concerns surfaced at final handoff for developer review.\\n\\n## Fact Check Results\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n### Claim 1: Final Handoff Output uses `backlog_list(title=\\\"{slug}\\\")`\\n**VERDICT: VERIFIED**\\n\\n**Evidence:**\\n- Source: `plugins/development-harness/skills/complete-implementation/references/final-handoff.md` lines 10-23\\n- The Mermaid flowchart shows the first step as: `backlog_list(title='{slug}')` with the description \\\"Slug-filtered search \u2014 mandatory, not substitutable\\\"\\n- This is the ONLY search mechanism in the Final Handoff Output procedure\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Claim 2: Final Handoff Output does NOT read Concerns section\\n**VERDICT: VERIFIED**\\n\\n**Evidence:**\\n- Source: `plugins/development-harness/skills/complete-implementation/references/final-handoff.md` lines 10-23\\n- The Final Handoff Output flowchart (the \\\"authoritative procedure\\\") contains no step that reads or references the Concerns section\\n- The procedure only:\\n  1. Calls `backlog_list(title='{slug}')`\\n  2. Checks if `item.plan` is set\\n  3. Dispatches to either SAM via `/dh:implement-feature` or `/dh:work-backlog-item`\\n- Concerns ARE processed in Pre-Phase 1b (line 344-348 of SKILL.md), but that occurs BEFORE quality gates and Final Handoff, not during it\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Claim 3: Concerns logged via `backlog_groom(section=\\\"Concerns\\\", ...)`\\n**VERDICT: VERIFIED**\\n\\n**Evidence:**\\n- Source: `plugins/development-harness/skills/complete-implementation/references/concerns-processing.md` line 22\\n- The procedure explicitly shows: `backlog_groom(selector='#{issue}', section='Concerns', content='{updated checklist}')`\\n- The section key is `\\\"Concerns\\\"` (uppercase C)\\n- This is called to update the Concerns checklist after processing each concern\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Claim 4: `backlog_view` can filter by section name\\n**VERDICT: VERIFIED**\\n\\n**Evidence:**\\n- Source: `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py` (backlog_view function definition)\\n- The `section` parameter accepts:\\n  - Numeric indices: `'2'`\\n  - Comma-separated indices: `'0,2,4'`\\n  - Regex patterns: `'/impact.*/'`\\n  - Substring matches: e.g., `'RT-ICA'` or `'Concerns'`\\n- When provided, the response body and sections reflect only the matched section(s)\\n- Note: \\\"Section filter for YAML items (no effect on GitHub-only items with a raw body)\\\"\\n\\n---\\n</div>\\n\\n## Summary\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAll four claims are VERIFIED against source code. The issue accurately describes the problem: Final Handoff uses title-based search only and never examines the Concerns section, causing concern-derived follow-on items to be silently dropped.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"fc57bdbfaafcc3c630e8b35239cc2223ff9afcdcf635c233ad9cb7a204c35ca8\",\"com